Choosing a wedding venue in Touraine starts with choosing a type of venue, well before comparing addresses. A château, a cave cut into the tuffeau, a barn or a riverside guinguette are not hired the same way, do not hold the same number of guests and do not cause the same problems on the day.

Here are the ten types of venue you find in Touraine, what each does well and what each makes you pay for. We are one of them, at Nazelles-Négron near Amboise, and we will say so when we are not the right fit.

## What are the 10 types of wedding venue in Touraine?

Touraine offers ten broad families of venues, from the château with bedrooms to the village hall, with capacities running from 30 to over 150 guests. The right choice depends less on the address than on the format you pick.

### 1. The château-hotel, open all year

It keeps a hotel business outside weddings, so bedrooms are ready, the kitchen is practised and staff are on site. That is our case: 10 bedrooms, 21 beds, 14 hectares of walled grounds. The point to check is exclusivity. Some hotels hire out a room without closing the rest, and you meet guests in dressing gowns during your drinks reception.

### 2. The reception château, without accommodation

The setting is the same, but nobody sleeps there. You will need a hotel for your guests and shuttles late at night. In return, these estates sometimes hold more guests, and their caterer is often imposed: ask for the list before falling in love with the room.

![Vaulted reception room set for a wedding, round tables with white cloths and gold chairs with burgundy seats](/images/inline/mariage/mariage-08.webp "A seated dinner for 100 guests needs at least 200 m², dance floor included: measure the room before you visit it.")

### 3. The manor house

Smaller and more intimate, with a garden rather than a park. It suits around fifty guests, rarely many more. The charm is real; the rain plan is less so, so ask about it.

### 4. The wine estate

Vouvray and Montlouis-sur-Loire are neighbours of Amboise. A drinks reception among the vines is a postcard that neither a château nor a hall can offer. The downside: vats and cellars take up half the premises, and the grape harvest blocks September and October. Our [guide to the Vouvray and Montlouis vineyards](/en/blog/vouvray-montlouis-wine-tour) names the appellations.

### 5. The troglodyte cave

The slopes of the Loire are cut into tuffeau, and some caves host dinners. The atmosphere is unique, and so is the temperature: it stays around 12 °C all year, so pack a shawl in July. Capacity rarely exceeds 50 guests, and dancing happens elsewhere.

### 6. The renovated farm or barn

Beams, stone, long tables: the rustic style is often rented at a gentle price. Check the heating in winter, the toilets and the time the sound must stop, which the neighbours set more than the owner.

### 7. The guinguette and the Loire riverbanks

In Tours and upstream, guinguettes open in summer by the water. It is the venue for relaxed weddings, and capacity depends on the establishment. Out of season the weather decides, and the season ends in September.

### 8. The village hall

It is the cheapest: hire is counted in hundreds of euros. Everything else adds up: tables, chairs, tableware, decoration, caterer, accommodation. In the end the gap with an all-inclusive exclusive-use château is often thinner than you would imagine. To measure it, read our piece on the [cost of a château wedding](/en/blog/chateau-wedding-cost).

### 9. The privately hired restaurant or hotel-restaurant

Ideal for 20 to 40 guests, with no logistics: the kitchen is there, and so is the room. You lose the space to dance and the freedom to stay until dawn. See our article on the [intimate wedding of 30 to 60 guests](/en/blog/intimate-wedding-chateau-loire).

### 10. The private house hired by the week

A large house, grounds, a weekend with close family. This format gives real freedom, and in exchange you manage everything: caterer, cleaning, beds, neighbours. It works for couples who like organising.

## How do you choose according to guest numbers?

Guest numbers settle the question faster than any other criterion: under 40 almost anything is possible, over 100 only properly sized estates cope. That rule already removes half of the ten families above.

For 30 to 50 guests, a manor house, a cave, a restaurant or a barn will do. From 50 to 100, wine estates, farms and guinguettes come into play. Beyond 100, that leaves châteaux and village halls. La Huberdière welcomes 30 to 150 guests, and no more: a venue advertising 300 places should prove it has held them before.

![Newlyweds embracing on mossy stone steps in the grounds, the veil spread across the steps](/images/inline/mariage/mariage-01.webp "Walled grounds allow couple portraits without leaving the estate, and without meeting anyone.")

## Which part of Touraine should you choose for a wedding?

Pick the area by the station and your guests' journey, not by the prettiest village name. Tours is 1 hr from Paris by TGV, via Saint-Pierre-des-Corps station, and Amboise is 30 minutes by car from it.

Around Amboise, the royal château, the Clos Lucé and the town's restaurants give guests something to do on Sunday. West of Tours there are more reception estates, but further from the stations. From Paris, allow 2 hrs 20 by road: it is the balance point between wanting a change of scene and a family's patience. The region has been a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 2000, which is no guarantee of quality: it simply makes for good scenery.

To house close family, our guide to [where to stay in Amboise](/en/blog/where-to-stay-amboise) goes through the options outside the château.

## How much does a wedding venue cost in Touraine?

An exclusive-use château most often rents between €4,000 and €8,000 for the weekend, a village hall a few hundred euros, and the rest of the bill depends on what the venue includes. What is included is what you compare, not the headline price.

At La Huberdière, exclusive use from Friday to Sunday runs from €4,000 to €8,000 depending on the season, beds included. Reception furniture is added at €15 per person, tables and chairs included. You can bring your own wines: when we provide the meal, corkage is €8 per bottle. With an outside caterer, their terms apply, so read the quote.

## What should you check before signing a wedding venue?

Five checks prevent almost every bad surprise: exclusivity, the exact number of beds, the caterer, the rain plan and the finishing time. Ask them in writing, not by phone.

- Are there other bookings or guests that weekend?
- How many beds, not bedrooms, are there on site?
- Is the caterer free, imposed, and what is the corkage?
- Which covered room takes over if it rains, for the same number of guests?
- What time does the music stop, and what time must the premises be vacated?

One legal point: a civil marriage is held at the town hall, never at the château, as [service-public.fr](https://www.service-public.fr) explains. The grounds then host the [humanist ceremony](/en/blog/humanist-wedding-ceremony-chateau), which has no legal value but is often the finest moment.

![Humanist ceremony on the gravel terrace of the château, guests seated on wrought-iron chairs facing blue and white hydrangeas](/images/inline/mariage/mariage-02.webp "The terrace hosts the ceremony facing the park, with the vaulted reception room as a fallback thirty seconds away.")

